                'alt' => 'Hail Damage Insurance Claim in Texas: A Step-by-Step Guide',
                'credit' => 'Photo: Bobby Ketchum / Flickr (CC0)',
                'credit_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/32671284@N08/11469471905',
            ],
            'body_html' => '<p>Hail is a fact of life across much of Texas, and a single storm can leave dents, cracked shingles, and broken windows across an entire neighborhood. What you do in the days after matters: a calm, well-documented claim tends to go far more smoothly than a rushed one. This guide walks you through it, from the first look outside to the final payment.</p>

<p>This is general information, not insurance or legal advice. Texas policies vary widely, so read your own policy and ask your insurer, a licensed public adjuster, or an attorney about your specific situation.</p>

<h2>Right after the storm: safety first</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wait until the storm has fully passed.</strong> Hail often comes with lightning, high winds, and sometimes tornadoes.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for hazards</strong>: downed power lines, broken glass, and hanging limbs. Treat any downed line as live and report it to your utility.</li>
<li><strong>Stay off the roof.</strong> Wet or damaged shingles are slippery, and falls are a real risk. Let a professional handle anything that requires climbing.</li>
<li><strong>Protect the inside</strong> if water is coming in: move belongings, set out buckets, and run towels or a wet/dry vacuum.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the storm knocked out your power, keep flashlights handy and avoid candles near damaged areas or anything wet.</p>

<h2>How to check for hail damage from the ground</h2>
<p>You can learn a lot without a ladder. Walk the property slowly and look for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Soft metals</strong>: dents in gutters, downspouts, roof vents, AC condenser fins, mailboxes, and window wraps. These are often the easiest signs to spot and photograph.</li>
<li><strong>Granules</strong> from asphalt shingles collecting at the bottom of downspouts or in gutters.</li>
<li><strong>Visible shingle damage</strong> through binoculars or a phone camera zoom: dark spots, cracks, missing pieces, or exposed mat.</li>
<li><strong>Siding and trim</strong>: cracks, holes, or chipped paint on the side facing the storm.</li>
<li><strong>Windows and screens</strong>: cracked panes and torn or dented screens.</li>
<li><strong>Fences, decks, patio furniture, and outdoor lights.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Interior clues</strong>: new ceiling stains or drips, especially in the attic after rain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Hail damage on a roof is not always obvious from below. If you see dented gutters or vents, it is reasonable to have the roof inspected by a reputable roofer or to wait for your insurer\'s adjuster.</p>

<h2>Document everything before cleanup</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Photograph the hail itself</strong> if you can, with a coin or ruler for scale, and note the date and time.</li>
<li><strong>Take wide shots of each side of the house</strong>, then close-ups of every dent, crack, or hole.</li>
<li><strong>Record a narrated video</strong> walking around the property.</li>
<li><strong>Save local weather reports</strong> or news coverage showing hail in your area on that date.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph interior water damage</strong> and damaged belongings. A <a href="/blog/home-inventory-for-insurance/">home inventory for insurance</a> makes the contents part much easier.</li>
<li><strong>Keep receipts</strong> for tarps, emergency repairs, and any temporary lodging.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Prevent further damage</h2>
<p>Most policies require you to take reasonable steps to protect your property after a loss. That might mean tarping a damaged roof section (by a professional), boarding a broken window, or drying out wet areas. Reasonable emergency repair costs are often reimbursable, so keep receipts. Hold off on permanent repairs, like a full roof replacement, until your insurer has inspected or agreed in writing. If water got inside, our <a href="/blog/water-damage-insurance-claim-tips/">water damage insurance claim tips</a> cover drying and documentation.</p>

<h2>Filing your claim promptly</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pull out your policy and declarations page.</strong> Look for your wind and hail deductible, roof coverage terms, and any cosmetic damage exclusions.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim soon after the storm.</strong> Check your policy for notice and proof-of-loss deadlines. Waiting can make it harder to connect damage to a specific storm.</li>
<li><strong>Get a claim number</strong> and your adjuster\'s name and contact details.</li>
<li><strong>Be there for the inspection</strong> if you can. Point out everything you found, including soft metal dents, interior stains, and outbuildings.</li>
<li><strong>Get an independent estimate</strong> from a qualified contractor so you can compare it with the insurer\'s.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a claim log</strong> of every call, email, and visit, with dates and names.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for written explanations</strong> of any denial or partial payment, including the policy language relied on.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Texas-specific things to know</h2>
<h3>Wind and hail deductibles</h3>
<p>Many Texas homeowners policies use a separate wind and hail deductible, often set as a percentage of your dwelling coverage rather than a flat dollar amount. A percentage deductible can be much larger than people expect, so do the math before the next storm season.</p>
<h3>Roof coverage terms</h3>
<p>Some Texas policies pay roof claims on an actual cash value basis, apply a roof payment schedule based on age, or exclude purely cosmetic hail damage (like dents in metal roofing that do not affect function). Check your policy for these provisions; they can change your payout significantly.</p>
<h3>Coastal windstorm coverage</h3>
<p>In designated coastal areas, wind and hail coverage may come from the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) instead of your homeowners policy, which means a separate policy and separate claim process.</p>
<h3>Deductibles and contractors</h3>
<p>Texas law prohibits contractors from offering to waive, absorb, or rebate your insurance deductible. Be cautious of anyone who promises a "free roof." You are responsible for paying your deductible.</p>
<h3>Where to get help</h3>
<p>The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> publishes consumer guidance on storm claims, can answer questions, and accepts complaints about insurers and adjusters. Weather risk also varies a lot across the state; our <a href="/blog/texas-climate-regions-guide/">Texas climate regions guide</a> explains why.</p>

<h2>Roofers and storm chasers: how to protect yourself</h2>
<p>After big hailstorms, out-of-town crews often go door to door. Many are legitimate, but some leave homeowners with poor work and no one to call. Texas does not have a statewide license for residential roofers, so your own vetting matters.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Prefer established local contractors</strong> with a physical address and references you can call.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for proof of insurance</strong>, including liability and workers\' compensation.</li>
<li><strong>Get written estimates</strong> from more than one contractor.</li>
<li><strong>Read before you sign.</strong> Be careful with contracts that give a contractor control of your claim or assign your insurance benefits to them.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid large upfront payments.</strong> Pay as work is completed, and keep records of every payment.</li>
<li><strong>Be wary of pressure</strong>: "today only" pricing, claims of a free roof, or a contractor offering to negotiate your claim. Texas law generally bars contractors from acting as public adjusters; a licensed public adjuster or an attorney can represent you on a claim.</li>
</ul>

<h2>If the payment seems too low</h2>
<ul>
<li>Compare the insurer\'s estimate line by line with your contractor\'s.</li>
<li>Ask for a re-inspection and send additional photos or evidence.</li>
<li>Review whether your policy\'s appraisal clause applies. See our <a href="/blog/insurance-appraisal-tips/">insurance appraisal tips</a>.</li>
<li>Consider a licensed public adjuster. Read <a href="/blog/how-public-adjusters-help-with-insurance-claims/">how public adjusters help with insurance claims</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can I file a hail claim if I did not notice damage right away?</h3>
<p>Possibly, but delays can make it harder to tie damage to a specific storm. Check your policy for notice deadlines and report as soon as you find damage.</p>
<h3>Will filing a hail claim raise my premium?</h3>
<p>It can depend on your insurer and claims history. Ask your agent or insurer how claims may affect your policy before you decide.</p>
<h3>Do renters need to file a hail claim?</h3>
<p>The landlord\'s policy usually covers the building. Renters may need their own renters insurance claim for damaged belongings or a car claim for a dented vehicle. See our guide to <a href="/blog/renters-storm-damage-claim/">renters storm damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Does homeowners insurance cover hail damage to my car?</h3>
<p>Generally no. Hail damage to a vehicle is typically handled under the comprehensive coverage of an auto policy.</p>

<p>Texas hail season brings a lot of shared experience. If you are mid-claim or just went through one, come compare notes with other Texans in our <a href="/forum/insurance-claims/">Insurance Claims community</a>.</p>',
