                'credit_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/6036495/photo-image-desktop-wallpaper-light-public-domain',
            ],
            'body_html' => '<p>After a fire, burglary, or burst pipe, your insurer will usually ask you to list what you lost, often item by item. Trying to remember everything in a stressful moment is hard, and whatever you forget, you generally do not get paid for. A home inventory done ahead of time solves that, and it takes less effort than most people think.</p>

<p>This guide works for renters and homeowners alike. It is general information, not insurance or legal advice, and every policy has its own documentation requirements.</p>

<h2>Why a home inventory matters</h2>
<p>A good inventory helps you in three ways:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Faster, smoother claims.</strong> You can hand your adjuster a list with photos instead of rebuilding your memory from scratch.</li>
<li><strong>Better coverage decisions.</strong> Once you see the total value of what you own, you can check whether your personal property limit is high enough.</li>
<li><strong>Proof of ownership and condition.</strong> Photos, receipts, and serial numbers support the value you claim and can help after a theft, too.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are not sure what your policy covers in the first place, start with <a href="/blog/what-does-renters-insurance-cover/">what renters insurance covers</a>. Homeowners policies follow a similar structure for personal property.</p>

<h2>What to record for each item</h2>
<p>You do not need every detail for every spoon. Focus your effort on items that would be costly or hard to replace. For those, try to capture:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Detail</th><th>Why it helps</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Description, brand, and model</td><td>Lets the adjuster price a comparable replacement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Serial number</td><td>Supports ownership and helps police after a theft</td></tr>
<tr><td>Approximate purchase date</td><td>Used to estimate depreciation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purchase price or estimated value</td><td>Gives a starting point for the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Photo or video</td><td>Shows the item existed and its condition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Receipt, appraisal, or order confirmation</td><td>Strongest proof of value, especially for high-value items</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Give high-value items extra attention</h3>
<p>Jewelry, watches, art, collectibles, firearms, and specialty electronics often have lower coverage limits under a standard policy. For these, keep appraisals or receipts with your inventory and ask your agent whether a scheduled personal property endorsement makes sense. Seeing the full list in one place is often what prompts people to ask that question for the first time.</p>
<p>For everyday items like clothing, books, and kitchenware, a photo of the open closet or cabinet plus a rough count is usually enough to jog your memory later.</p>

<h2>Step by step: how to create your inventory</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pick your tool.</strong> A simple spreadsheet, a notes app, a dedicated home inventory app, or a printed checklist all work. The best tool is the one you will actually finish.</li>
<li><strong>Start with one room.</strong> Do not try to do the whole house in a day. One room per evening is a realistic pace.</li>
<li><strong>Record a walkthrough video.</strong> Narrate as you go: "Living room, 65-inch TV, bought about two years ago." Open drawers, closets, and cabinets.</li>
<li><strong>Take close-up photos</strong> of valuable items, labels, and serial number plates.</li>
<li><strong>Add the details</strong> from the table above for anything costly.</li>
<li><strong>Gather proof of value.</strong> Search your email and online store accounts for order confirmations. Snap photos of paper receipts before they fade.</li>
<li><strong>Back it all up off-site</strong> (more on that below).</li>
<li><strong>Set a reminder</strong> to update it.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Room-by-room checklist</h2>
<p>Use this as a prompt list so you do not overlook whole categories.</p>
<h3>Living room</h3>
<ul>
<li>TV, streaming devices, sound system, gaming consoles and games</li>
<li>Sofa, chairs, tables, rugs, lamps</li>
<li>Artwork, décor, books, media collections</li>
</ul>
<h3>Kitchen and dining</h3>
<ul>
<li>Small appliances: mixer, blender, coffee maker, air fryer, microwave</li>
<li>Cookware, knives, dishes, glassware, flatware</li>
<li>Dining table and chairs, pantry stock</li>
<li>For homeowners: refrigerator, range, dishwasher (note make and model)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Bedrooms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mattresses, bed frames, dressers, nightstands</li>
<li>Clothing, shoes, coats, handbags (photograph closets with doors open)</li>
<li>Jewelry and watches (close-ups, plus appraisals if you have them)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Bathrooms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Towels and linens, hair tools, electric toothbrushes, scales</li>
<li>Toiletries and cosmetics add up more than you might think</li>
</ul>
<h3>Home office</h3>
<ul>
<li>Computers, monitors, printers, phones, tablets, cameras</li>
<li>Desk, chair, shelving, external drives</li>
<li>Note any business equipment separately, since many personal policies limit it</li>
</ul>
<h3>Garage, closets, and storage</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tools, ladders, lawn equipment, bikes, sports gear</li>
<li>Holiday decorations, camping gear, luggage</li>
<li>Items in a storage unit (check how your policy treats off-site storage)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Photo and video tips that make a difference</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Use good light</strong> and shoot wide first, then close-up.</li>
<li><strong>Show brand labels and model tags</strong>, which are often on the back or bottom.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the original files.</strong> Phone photos usually carry a date stamp in their file data, which helps show when the photo was taken.</li>
<li><strong>Capture condition.</strong> A clear shot of a well-kept item helps if depreciation is discussed later.</li>
<li><strong>Do not forget the structure</strong> if you own your home: flooring, cabinets, fixtures, and upgrades. Those photos can help with dwelling claims too.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where to store your inventory</h2>
<p>An inventory kept only on a laptop in the house can be lost in the same fire or flood it was meant to document. Keep at least one copy off-site:</p>
<ul>
<li>A cloud storage account or the backup built into your inventory app</li>
<li>An email to yourself with the spreadsheet and a link to the photo folder</li>
<li>A USB drive at a relative\'s home, at work, or in a safe deposit box</li>
<li>Paper copies of key receipts and appraisals in a fire- and water-resistant pouch</li>
</ul>
<p>It also helps to store a copy of your insurance policy and declarations page in the same place, along with your insurer\'s claim phone number.</p>

<h2>Keep it current without a big project</h2>
<ul>
<li>Add big purchases the day you unbox them: snap a photo and save the receipt.</li>
<li>Do a quick review once a year. Some people tie it to a memorable date, like the policy renewal.</li>
<li>Update after moving, remodeling, or receiving valuable gifts.</li>
<li>Recheck your coverage limits whenever your total grows noticeably.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Using your inventory when you file a claim</h2>
<p>If you have a loss, your inventory becomes the backbone of your claim:</p>
<ol>
<li>Report the claim and ask the adjuster what format they want for the contents list.</li>
<li>Mark which items were damaged or lost and photograph the damage.</li>
<li>Do not throw away damaged items until the adjuster says it is OK, unless they create a safety hazard. If you must, photograph them first.</li>
<li>Keep a copy of everything you send, and check your policy for notice and proof-of-loss deadlines.</li>
</ol>
<p>Contents claims after large losses can get complicated. If the numbers are far apart, read about <a href="/blog/how-public-adjusters-help-with-insurance-claims/">how public adjusters help with insurance claims</a> and the <a href="/blog/insurance-appraisal-tips/">insurance appraisal process</a>. The <a href="https://www.iii.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Information Institute</a> also offers consumer resources on home inventories.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is a video enough for a home inventory?</h3>
<p>A narrated video is a great start and far better than nothing. Adding a written list with serial numbers and values for expensive items makes it much stronger.</p>
<h3>What if I do not have receipts?</h3>
<p>Photos, bank or card statements, online order history, owner\'s manuals, and even photos of the item in the background of family pictures can help support ownership and value.</p>
<h3>How long does a home inventory take?</h3>
<p>It depends on your home, but spreading it out one room at a time keeps it manageable. A quick video walkthrough can be done in a single session.</p>
<h3>Should renters make an inventory too?</h3>
<p>Yes. Renters often own more than they realize, and an inventory helps you choose the right coverage limit and file a complete claim.</p>

<p>Have a system that works for you, or a lesson learned from a past claim? Share it in our <a href="/forum/insurance-claims/">Insurance Claims community</a> so other renters and homeowners can learn from it. For help with a specific claim, your insurer, a licensed public adjuster, or an attorney can advise you.</p>',
