            ],
            'body_html' => '<p>Your landlord\'s insurance protects the building, not your couch, your laptop, or you. Renters insurance fills that gap, and it usually costs less than people expect. Here is what a typical policy covers, what it usually leaves out, and the few settings that make the biggest difference when you actually file a claim.</p>

<p>This is general information, not insurance or legal advice. Policies vary by company and by state, so your declarations page and policy wording always have the final say.</p>

<h2>The three main parts of a renters policy</h2>
<p>Most standard renters policies (often called an HO-4 form) are built around three core coverages. Each one has its own limit listed on your declarations page.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Personal property</strong> pays to repair or replace your belongings after a covered loss.</li>
<li><strong>Personal liability</strong> helps if you are legally responsible for injuring someone or damaging their property.</li>
<li><strong>Loss of use</strong> (also called additional living expenses) helps with extra costs if your rental becomes unlivable after a covered loss.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many policies also include <strong>medical payments to others</strong>, a small coverage that can pay minor medical bills if a guest is hurt, regardless of fault.</p>

<h2>Personal property: your stuff, at home and away</h2>
<p>Personal property coverage applies to the things you own: furniture, clothing, electronics, kitchenware, bikes, and so on. It generally pays when the damage comes from a "covered peril." Common named perils in renters policies include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Fire, smoke, and lightning</li>
<li>Windstorm and hail</li>
<li>Theft and vandalism</li>
<li>Explosion</li>
<li>Sudden and accidental water discharge, such as a burst pipe or an overflowing water heater</li>
<li>Weight of ice, snow, or sleet</li>
<li>Damage from vehicles or aircraft</li>
</ul>
<p>Coverage often follows your belongings off-premises too. A laptop stolen from your car or luggage taken from a hotel room may be covered, though some policies limit off-premises coverage to a percentage of your total limit.</p>

<h3>Watch for special limits</h3>
<p>Policies commonly cap certain categories, such as jewelry, watches, furs, firearms, collectibles, cash, and sometimes electronics or business property. If you own high-value items, ask about a scheduled personal property endorsement (sometimes called a rider or floater). It lists specific items at agreed values and can broaden the causes of loss that are covered.</p>

<h2>Liability: protection when you are responsible</h2>
<p>Liability coverage is the part people forget, and it can matter more than the property side. If a guest trips on your rug and gets hurt, or your bathtub overflows and damages the unit below, liability coverage can help pay for the other person\'s damages and your legal defense, up to your limit.</p>
<p>It generally does not cover injuries to you or members of your household, damage you cause on purpose, or business activities. Dog bites are often covered, but some insurers restrict certain breeds or exclude animal liability, so disclose pets when you buy the policy.</p>
<p>If you share a unit, remember that a roommate usually is not covered under your policy unless they are named on it. Our guide to <a href="/blog/roommates-renters-insurance-claim/">roommates and renters insurance claims</a> walks through how that plays out.</p>

<h2>Loss of use: if you cannot stay in your home</h2>
<p>If a covered loss, like a kitchen fire, makes your apartment uninhabitable, loss of use coverage can pay the <strong>difference</strong> between your normal living costs and what you are spending now. That can include a hotel or short-term rental, higher food costs from eating out, extra mileage, or laundry.</p>
<p>A few practical points:</p>
<ul>
<li>It is about <strong>additional</strong> costs. If you normally spend a certain amount on groceries, the policy generally pays only the increase.</li>
<li>Keep every receipt and a simple log of dates and expenses.</li>
<li>Ask your adjuster before booking expensive lodging so you know what is considered reasonable.</li>
<li>Some policies cap loss of use at a dollar amount or a time limit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What renters insurance usually does not cover</h2>
<p>Exclusions are where most claim surprises come from. Common ones include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Flood.</strong> Standard renters policies generally exclude flood, including storm surge and rising water. Renters can buy contents-only flood coverage through the National Flood Insurance Program or private insurers. <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FloodSmart.gov</a> explains the NFIP options.</li>
<li><strong>Earthquake and earth movement.</strong> Usually excluded, but often available as an endorsement or separate policy.</li>
<li><strong>Gradual damage and neglect.</strong> Slow leaks, mold from long-term moisture, and wear and tear are typically excluded.</li>
<li><strong>Pests.</strong> Damage from bed bugs, rodents, and insects is commonly excluded.</li>
<li><strong>The building itself.</strong> Walls, floors, and built-in appliances are generally the landlord\'s responsibility, though some policies offer limited coverage for improvements you paid for.</li>
<li><strong>Your car.</strong> The vehicle belongs on an auto policy, although personal items stolen from it may fall under renters coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Sewer or drain backup.</strong> Often excluded unless you add a water backup endorsement.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a deeper look at storms specifically, see <a href="/blog/renters-storm-damage-claim/">how renters handle storm damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Actual cash value vs. replacement cost</h2>
<p>This one setting can change your check more than almost anything else. It decides how your belongings are valued after a loss.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th></th><th>Actual cash value (ACV)</th><th>Replacement cost (RCV)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>What it pays</td><td>Cost to replace, minus depreciation</td><td>Cost to replace with a new, similar item</td></tr>
<tr><td>Example: a five-year-old TV</td><td>What that used TV is worth today</td><td>The price of a comparable new TV</td></tr>
<tr><td>Premium</td><td>Lower</td><td>Somewhat higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>How payment often works</td><td>One depreciated payment</td><td>ACV first, then the rest after you replace the item and submit proof</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Replacement cost coverage is usually worth the modest extra premium. If you have it, keep your receipts when you buy replacements, because many policies require proof before releasing the withheld depreciation, and some set a time limit for doing so.</p>

<h2>How to choose coverage that actually fits</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Estimate what you own.</strong> Walk room by room and add up what it would cost to replace everything. Most people underestimate. A <a href="/blog/home-inventory-for-insurance/">home inventory for insurance</a> makes this easier and speeds up any future claim.</li>
<li><strong>Pick a personal property limit</strong> that covers that total.</li>
<li><strong>Choose replacement cost</strong> if it fits your budget.</li>
<li><strong>Set liability high enough</strong> to protect your savings and future income. Higher limits often add relatively little to the premium.</li>
<li><strong>Choose a deductible</strong> you could comfortably pay tomorrow.</li>
<li><strong>Add endorsements</strong> for valuables, water backup, or earthquake if they apply to you.</li>
<li><strong>Ask about flood</strong> if you live anywhere water can reach, not just in a mapped flood zone.</li>
</ol>
<p>The <a href="https://content.naic.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Association of Insurance Commissioners</a> also publishes consumer guides that explain coverage terms and how to compare policies.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Does renters insurance cover water damage?</h3>
<p>It often covers sudden and accidental water damage, such as a burst pipe or overflowing appliance. Flood, gradual leaks, and sewer backup are usually excluded unless you add specific coverage. See our <a href="/blog/water-damage-insurance-claim-tips/">water damage insurance claim tips</a> for details.</p>
<h3>Does my landlord\'s insurance cover my belongings?</h3>
<p>Generally no. A landlord\'s policy typically covers the building and the landlord\'s liability, not tenants\' personal property or tenants\' liability.</p>
<h3>Are my roommate\'s things covered under my policy?</h3>
<p>Usually not unless they are named on your policy. Many insurers recommend each roommate carry their own policy to keep claims and limits separate.</p>
<h3>Does renters insurance cover theft outside my home?</h3>
<p>Many policies cover belongings stolen away from home, sometimes with a lower limit. Check the off-premises section of your policy.</p>

<p>If you are weighing policies or working through a claim, you do not have to figure it out alone. For specific situations, your insurer, a licensed public adjuster, or an attorney can help. And if you want to hear how other renters handled theirs, come compare notes in our <a href="/forum/insurance-claims/">Insurance Claims community</a>.</p>',
